T*
(configuration: # On sender ... ip address add dev eth0 fd42:dead:beef::1/48 ip link set dev) Tj
T*
(eth0 up # On receiver ... ip address add dev eth0 fd42:dead:beef::2/48 ip link set dev eth0 up) Tj
T*
(After a few seconds, pings should work: # On receiver ... / # ping fd42:dead:beef::1 64 bytes) Tj
T*
(from fd42:dead:beef::1: icmp_seq=1 ttl=64 time=0.649 ms 64 bytes from fd42:dead:beef::1:) Tj
T*
(icmp_seq=2 ttl=64 time=0.376 ms 64 bytes from fd42:dead:beef::1: icmp_seq=3 ttl=64 time=0.414) Tj
T*
(ms 64 bytes from fd42:dead:beef::1: icmp_seq=4 ttl=64 time=0.340 ms ... and so should this: #) Tj
T*
(On receiver ... socat - TCP6-LISTEN: 1234 | dd status = progress > big_file.tar.gz # On sender) Tj
T*
(... socat - 'TCP6-CONNECT: [fd42:dead:beef::2] : 1234 ' big_file.tar.gz These commands assume) Tj
T*
(you are using Linux, but this trick works everywhere. A nothing-special patch cable and) Tj
T*
(ethernet jack should have no problem hitting ~900 Mbits/second, which is 6.7 GB per minute.) Tj
T*
